          "buttonOnMobile": false,
          "label": "<p>Which of the following is a recognised limitation of chorionic villus sampling t</p>",
          "x": 30,
          "y": 30,
          "width": 40,
          "height": 25,
          "libraryTitle": "Multiple Choice",
          "action": {
            "library": "H5P.MultiChoice 1.16",
            "params": {
              "question": "<p>Which of the following is a recognised limitation of chorionic villus sampling that is not shared by amniocentesis?</p>",
              "answers": [
                {
                  "text": "<div>Risk of pregnancy loss following the procedure</div>",
                  "correct": false,
                  "tipsAndFeedback": {
                    "tip": "",
                    "chosenFeedback": "<div>Both amniocentesis and CVS carry a risk of pregnancy loss. CVS has a slightly higher rate (1–2 per cent) compared to amniocentesis (0.1–0.5 per cent), but this risk is common to both procedures.</div>",
                    "notChosenFeedback": ""
                  }
                },
                {
                  "text": "<div>Requirement for continuous ultrasound guidance during sampling</div>",
                  "correct": false,
                  "tipsAndFeedback": {
                    "tip": "",
                    "chosenFeedback": "<div>Both amniocentesis and CVS are performed under continuous ultrasound guidance. This is standard practice for both procedures.</div>",
                    "notChosenFeedback": ""
                  }
                },
                {
                  "text": "<div>Inability to detect open neural tube defects</div>",
                  "correct": true,
                  "tipsAndFeedback": {
                    "tip": "",
                    "chosenFeedback": "<div>Correct. CVS samples chorionic villi from the placenta, so no amniotic fluid is obtained. Detection of open neural tube defects relies on alpha-fetoprotein measurement in amniotic fluid, which is available only through amniocentesis.</div>",
                    "notChosenFeedback": ""
                  }
                },
                {
                  "text": "<div>A two-week waiting period before results are available</div>",
                  "correct": false,
                  "tipsAndFeedback": {
                    "tip": "",
                    "chosenFeedback": "<div>CVS actually offers faster results than amniocentesis. A direct preparation yields results in 24–48 hours, compared to the 10–14 day culture period typical of amniocentesis.</div>",
                    "notChosenFeedback": ""
                  }
                }
              ],
              "overallFeedback": [
                {
                  "from": 0,
                  "to": 50,
                  "feedback": "CVS samples chorionic villi from the placenta without collecting amniotic fluid. Since detecting open neural tube defects relies on alpha-fetoprotein measurement in amniotic fluid, CVS cannot identify these conditions — a limitation unique to this procedure."
                },
                {
                  "from": 51,
                  "to": 100,
                  "feedback": "Correct. Because CVS samples placental tissue and does not collect amniotic fluid, it cannot screen for open neural tube defects — a key limitation that distinguishes it from amniocentesis."
                }
              ],
